#
# The Appliance Repair Console on tty1: the last door.
#
# Packaged, not enabled: there is no [Install] section, so installing the package starts
# nothing at boot and leaves the login prompt on tty1 exactly where it was. Enabling it is
# the image's decision, and Conflicts= below says what that decision costs — this console
# takes tty1 from getty@tty1.service.
#
# WHY IT IS A UNIT OF ITS OWN AND DEPENDS ON NOTHING OF THE CONSOLE ON 9443. It exists for
# the state in which that console admits nobody: the product down and that console's
# sign-in stack unusable. A door that shares a failure with the door it stands in for is not
# a second door, so this unit names neither olivares-portal.socket nor
# olivares-portal.service in any ordering or requirement, and the service manager brings it
# up whether either of them is running or not. It is not a network service at all:
# PrivateNetwork=yes and IPAddressDeny=any stay in force. AF_UNIX carries helper requests;
# AF_NETLINK lets the host sign-in stack submit audit records. No IP socket family is allowed.
#
# WHY IT HOLDS NO CREDENTIAL STORE AND NAMES NO USER. It authenticates its operator by its
# own sign-in, through the host's local-console sign-in service, never by access to this
# machine's console, and it holds no credential store of its own, because a second credential
# store on an appliance is the thing this layer refuses to have. Access to the console alone
# authorizes no act: until its qualified sign-in exists, it performs none. What it adds is a
# bounded menu of named verbs instead of a shell.
#
# WHY THE HARDENING STOPS HERE. Every directive below is one this surface can carry for
# good. The ones that would have to be weakened when a repair verb arrives — the console
# must be able to hand an act to a privileged helper — are deliberately absent rather than
# set now and undone later: a directive that a later change removes reads, in the history,
# exactly like a hardening being lost.
#
# Power, certificate and firewall restore operations leave through their root-owned helper
# sockets. Wants and After start those sockets with this console; a socket failure does not
# remove the sign-in surface. The helper admits the console by its pidfd, cgroup and controlling
# terminal. Neither the package nor a preset enables these root sockets. Real sign-in and
# recovery behavior require guest qualification.
[Unit]
Description=Appliance Repair Console on tty1 (the last door)
Documentation=https://github.com/olivaresai/olivares
Conflicts=getty@tty1.service
Wants=olivares-helper-power.socket olivares-helper-cert.socket olivares-helper-firewall-local.socket
After=getty@tty1.service olivares-helper-power.socket olivares-helper-cert.socket olivares-helper-firewall-local.socket

[Service]
Type=idle
ExecStart=/usr/libexec/olivares/olivares-repair-console
StandardInput=tty
StandardOutput=tty
StandardError=journal
TTYPath=/dev/tty1
TTYReset=yes
TTYVHangup=yes
TTYVTDisallocate=yes
UtmpIdentifier=tty1
Restart=always
RestartSec=1
PrivateNetwork=yes
IPAddressDeny=any
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_NETLINK
LockPersonality=true
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=true
RestrictRealtime=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native
